        "prefix_token_count": 72,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Identify the direction vectors of both lines.\nThe direction vector of the first line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\nThe direction vector of the second line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 532,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 533,
        "char_start": 239,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 155,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 156,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Check if the lines are indeed parallel.\nNotice that the second direction vector is a scalar multiple of the first direction vector. Specifically, \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} = 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This confirms that the lines are parallel."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 759,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 760,
        "char_start": 535,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 244,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 245,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Find a point on each line.\nA point on the first line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (when \\(t = 0\\)).\nA point on the second line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (when \\(u = 0\\))."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1060,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1062,
        "char_start": 762,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 361,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 362,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: Calculate the vector connecting these two points.\nThe vector connecting the points \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -5 - 3 \\\\ -3 + 10 \\\\ 6 - 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1492,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1494,
        "char_start": 1064,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 487,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 488,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Use the formula for the distance between two parallel lines.\nThe distance \\(d\\) between two parallel lines can be found using the formula:\n\\[ d = \\frac{\\| \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} \\|}{\\| \\mathbf{c} \\|}, \\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) is the vector connecting a point on each line, \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is the direction vector of the lines, and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) is the direction vector of the lines (which is the same for both lines)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1989,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1990,
        "char_start": 1496,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 727,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 728,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: Compute the cross product \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\).\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -8 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -9 & -2 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(7 \\cdot -2 - 5 \\cdot -9) - \\mathbf{j}(-8 \\cdot -2 - 5 \\cdot 2) + \\mathbf{k}(-8 \\cdot -9 - 7 \\cdot 2)\n= \\mathbf{i}(-14 + 45) - \\mathbf{j}(16 - 10) + \\mathbf{k}(72 - 14)\n= \\begin{pmatrix} 31 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 58 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 2171,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 2172,
