  "raw_text": "Step 1: Identify the direction vectors of both lines.\nThe direction vector of the first line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\nThe direction vector of the second line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nStep 2: Check if the lines are indeed parallel.\nNotice that the second direction vector is a scalar multiple of the first direction vector. Specifically, \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} = 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This confirms that the lines are parallel.\n\nStep 3: Find a point on each line.\nA point on the first line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (when \\(t = 0\\)).\nA point on the second line is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (when \\(u = 0\\)).\n\nStep 4: Calculate the vector connecting these two points.\nThe vector connecting the points \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -5 - 3 \\\\ -3 + 10 \\\\ 6 - 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nStep 5: Use the formula for the distance between two parallel lines.\nThe distance \\(d\\) between two parallel lines can be found using the formula:\n\\[ d = \\frac{\\| \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} \\|}{\\| \\mathbf{c} \\|}, \\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) is the vector connecting a point on each line, \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is the direction vector of the lines, and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) is the direction vector of the lines (which is the same for both lines).\n\nStep 6: Compute the cross product \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\).\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -8 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -9 & -2 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(7 \\cdot -2 - 5 \\cdot -9) - \\mathbf{j}(-8 \\cdot -2 - 5 \\cdot 2) + \\mathbf{k}(-8 \\cdot -9 - 7 \\cdot 2)\n= \\mathbf{i}(-14 + 45) - \\mathbf{j}(16 - 10) + \\mathbf{k}(72 - 14)\n= \\begin{pmatrix} 31 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 58 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nStep 7: Calculate the magnitude of the cross product.\n\\[\n\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 31 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 58 \\end{pmatrix} \\| = \\sqrt{31^2 + (-6)^2 + 58^2} = \\sqrt{961 + 36 + 3364} = \\sqrt{4361}.\n\\]\n\nStep 8: Calculate the magnitude of the direction vector.\n\\[\n\\| \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\| = \\sqrt{2^2 + (-9)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 81 + 4} = \\sqrt{89}.\n\\]\n\nStep 9: Compute the distance.\n\\[\nd = \\frac{\\sqrt{4361}}{\\sqrt{89}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4361}{89}} = \\sqrt{49} = 7.\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{7}<|im_end|>",
